{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /nix/store/za7xbmc46hxs86r79kzmi4an7ff3616d-hm_skills/../za7xbmc46hxs86r79kzmi4an7ff3616d-hm_skills 2>/dev/null || true; cat /local/home/scheschb/.codex/plugins/cache/aim/AmazonBuilderCoreAIAgents-core/1.0.6505391367/skills/brazil/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: brazil\ndescription: Amazon's internal build system for Java/Kotlin (brazil-gradle), TypeScript/JS (npm-pretty-much), Python (brazil-python), Rust (CargoBrazil), Go, C/C++ (brazil-cmake). Use when working with Brazil packages, workspaces, dependencies, version sets, build commands, or encountering build errors and dependency conflicts.\nversion: 1.0.0\ntags: [skill, brazil, build-system, dependencies, java, kotlin, typescript, javascript, python, go, rust, c-cpp, cargobrazil, cargo-brazil]\n---\n\n# Brazil Build System\n\n## Overview\nBrazil is Amazon's internal build system providing a unified interface for diverse build technologies (npm-pretty-much, brazil-gradle, brazil-python, etc.). It manages dependencies, coordinates package versions through version sets, and supports reproducible builds across languages.\n\n**Main Documentation:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/\n\n## Usage\nWhen working with Amazon internal development and encountering:\n- Package creation, configuration, or dependency management\n- Workspace setup or build errors\n- Version conflicts or dependency resolution issues\n- Language-specific build system configuration\n- Publishing packages or setting up CI/CD\n- Commands like `brazil-build`, `brazil workspace`, `brazil versionset`\n\n## Quick Start\n\n### 1. Identify the Language First\nCheck the package's `Config` file for `build-system` to determine which language guide to reference:\n\n- `brazil-gradle`, `happytrails`, `happiertrails` → **[Java/Kotlin](references/language/java-kotlin.md)**\n- `npm-pretty-much` → **[TypeScript/JavaScript](references/language/typescript-javascript.md)**\n- `brazil-python` → **[Python](references/language/python.md)**\n- `brazil-cmake` → **[C/C++](references/language/c-cpp.md)**\n- `bgo-wrap-make` → **[Go](references/language/go.md)**\n- `cargo-brazil-build` → **[Rust](references/language/rust.md)**\n\nYou **MUST** reference the appropriate language guide for language-specific commands, dependencies, and troubleshooting.\n\n### 2. Reference Workflow Guides as Needed\n- **[Workspace Setup](references/workspace-setup.md)** - Creating workspaces, using packages, workspace structure\n- **[Package Management](references/package-management.md)** - Creating packages, configuring dependencies, publishing\n- **[Dependency Management](references/dependency-management.md)** - Resolving conflicts, debugging dependencies, Gordian Knot\n- **[Build and Test](references/build-and-test.md)** - Building packages, running tests, build output\n- **[Version Sets](references/version-sets.md)** - Creating or managing version sets\n- **[CLI Commands](references/cli-commands.md)** - Comprehensive Brazil CLI command reference\n\n## Core Concepts\n\n### Packages\nThe basic unit of code in Brazil containing source code, build configuration, and dependency information.\n\n**Key characteristics:**\n- Each package is a separate git repository\n- Lives in workspace `src/` directory\n- Owned by teams via bindles, not individuals\n\n**For details on package structure and Config files:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-package/ and https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-config-file/\n\n### Workspaces\nContainer for one or more packages you're working on, providing isolation and controlled build environment.\n\n**Structure:**\n```\nWorkspaceName/\n├── src/\n│   ├── PackageA/  (git repo)\n│   └── PackageB/  (git repo)\n└── [workspace metadata]\n```\n\n**Critical rule:** For single-package builds, you **MUST** navigate into the specific package directory (`cd src/PackageName`) before running build commands. For building all packages, use `brazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build` from any package in the Brazil workspace instead.\n\n### Version Sets\nCollections of package versions that are known to work together, enabling consistent builds across environments.\n\n**Purpose:**\n- Define package versions that work together\n- Coordinate dependency updates across teams\n- Enable consistent builds\n\n**For details:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-version-set/\n\n### Dependencies\nBrazil manages package dependencies to ensure required code is available during build and runtime.\n\n**Dependency Models:**\n- **Classic Brazil**: Dependencies declared in Config file - https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/\n- **Peru**: Language-native dependency management (e.g., package.json for npm, requirements.txt for Python) - https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/peru-user-guide/\n\n**Types:**\n- **Regular dependencies**: Required for compilation/runtime, exported to consumers\n- **Test dependencies**: Only for testing, not exported\n- **Runtime dependencies**: Required at runtime but not compile time\n- **Conflict resolution**: Use `resolves-conflict-dependencies` to specify versions when conflicts occur\n\n**For details:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-dependencies/\n\n### Build Systems\nBrazil supports multiple language-specific build systems. The most common are:\n- **Java/Kotlin**: brazil-gradle, happytrails/happiertrails\n- **TypeScript/JavaScript**: npm-pretty-much\n- **Python**: brazil-python\n- **Ruby**: brazil-rake\n- **Go**: bgo-wrap-make\n- **Rust**: brazil-cargo\n- **C/C++**: brazil-cmake\n- **Scala**: brazil-sbt, happiertrails\n\nSome packages use wrapper build systems (e.g., `cdk-build`) or fully custom ones (e.g., `custom-build`). If the build system is not recognized, check the package README or ask the user for guidance on how to build.\n\n## Common Workflows\n\n### Building an Existing Package in a workspace\n**When user already has a workspace with the package:**\n1. Confirm you are in a brazil workspace with `brazil workspace show`\n2. Navigate to package: `cd src/PackageName`\n3. Build: `brazil-build release`\n\n**To build all packages at once with the release target** (note: the same can be done with other targets like `test`):\n```bash\nbrazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build release\n```\n\n### Creating a New Workspace from Scratch and building\n**Only when creating a new workspace:**\n1. Find a package's primary version set at https://code.amazon.com/packages/PackageName/releases using ReadInternalWebsite\n2. Create workspace: `brazil workspace create --name MyWorkspace --versionSet MyVersionSetGroup/MyVersionSetName`. You **MUST** specify a version set, ask the user if you cannot find a primary version set.\n3. Use package: `brazil workspace use --package PackageName`\n4. Navigate to package: `cd src/PackageName`\n5. Build: `brazil-build release`\n\n### Resolving Dependency Conflicts\n1. Identify conflict via build error\n2. Use Gordian Knot: `gk-fix-mv-conflicts`\n3. Or manually add to Config:\n   ```\n   resolves-conflict-dependencies = {\n       1.0 = {\n           ConflictPackage = 2.x;\n       };\n   };\n   ```\n4. Rebuild: `brazil-build release`\n\n### Adding Dependencies\n1. Find package: https://code.amazon.com/packages/search\n2. Find package major version number at https://code.amazon.com/packages/PackageName/releases using ReadInternalWebsite\n3. Add to Config file dependencies section\n4. If version set errors occur: first try `brazil ws sync -md`, then `brazil workspace merge` if still needed\n5. Build: `brazil-build release`\n\n## CLI Validation Rules\n\nBefore executing any `brazil*` command, check:\n\n1. **Command exists**: Known `brazil` top-level subcommands include: `workspace`/`ws`, `versionset`/`vs`, `package`/`pkg`, `packagebuilder`/`pb`, `prefs`, `s3binary`/`s3`, `sandbox-build`, `setup`, `version`. Do NOT invent subcommands like `brazil merge`. If unsure whether a subcommand exists, run `brazil --help` to verify.\n2. **Correct nesting**: It's `brazil workspace merge`, NOT `brazil merge`. It's `brazil versionset print`, NOT `brazil print`.\n3. **Flag validity**: When unsure if a flag exists, run `<command> --help` first. Do NOT invent flags.\n4. **Working directory**: `brazil-build`, `brazil-path`, and `brazil-context package` subcommands MUST run from inside a package directory. `brazil ws` commands work from anywhere in the workspace.\n5. **Build system awareness**: `brazil-build` has NO flags of its own — it passes all arguments to the build system. The first argument is often called the \"target\" (e.g., in `brazil-build release`, `release` is the target). Do NOT use flag syntax like `brazil-build --target release`.\n6. **Interactive commands**: `brazil setup` (all variants) and `brazil ws delete` require interactive input and will hang or prompt in automated environments. Do NOT run these commands directly — instruct the user to run them manually in their terminal. See the \"Interactive Commands\" section for the full list.\n\n## Interactive Commands (Do NOT Run Directly)\n\nThese commands require interactive input and MUST NOT be run by the agent:\n\n- `brazil setup` (all variants: `--java`, `--node`, `--python`, `--all`, `--core`)\n- `brazil setup platform-support` — interactive by default. Instruct the user to run it in their terminal with `--force --mode overlay` (macOS) or `--force --mode legacy` (Windows) to skip prompts.\n- `brazil ws delete` — prompts for confirmation (warn user about data loss)\n\nThe agent MAY run `brazil ws remove --force -p <pkg>` only after the user has explicitly confirmed they want the package removed.\n\nWhen the user needs to run any interactive command, instruct them to run it manually in their terminal.\n\n## Output Handling Rules\n\n| Tool | Handling |\n|---|---|\n| `brazil-build`, `brazil-recursive-cmd` | Redirect to log file, tail last 50 lines |\n| `brazil-runtime-exec`, `brazil-farm` | Redirect to log file, tail last 50 lines |\n| `brazil-package-cache` | Read stdout directly (small JSON); check stderr on failure |\n| `brazil ws`, `brazil vs`, `brazil pkg`, `brazil pb`, `brazil-context`, `brazil-path` | Read output directly |\n\n## Decision Tree: Problem → Command\n\n| Problem | Command |\n|---|---|\n| Build single package | `brazil-build release` (from package dir) |\n| Build all packages | `brazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build release` |\n| Find workspace root | `brazil-context workspace root` |\n| Find package name | `brazil-context package name` |\n| Check dependency model (brazil vs peru) | `brazil-context workspace model` |\n| Find current platform | `brazil-context workspace platform` |\n| View build dependency graph | `brazil-path build.show-graph` (from package dir) |\n| View runtime dependency graph | `brazil-path run.show-graph` (from package dir) |\n| Java classpath | `brazil-path build.classpath` (from package dir) |\n| Python path | `brazil-path build.pythonpath.python<version>` (from package dir, e.g. `python3.11`) |\n| Add missing dependency | Edit Config, then `brazil ws sync -md`, then `brazil ws merge` if still needed |\n| Resolve version conflicts | `gk-fix-mv-conflicts` (requires Gordian Knot — if command not found, tell user to install via `toolbox install gordian-knot`) |\n| Is package in version set? | `brazil vs print --versionSet <VS>` and grep, or `brazil pkg listversionsets --package <Pkg> --majorVersion <MV>` |\n| Check build request status | `brazil pb view --buildRequest <id>` |\n| Submit dry-run build | `brazil ws dryrun` |\n| Sync workspace to latest | `brazil ws sync` |\n| Run cmd in runtime env (classic) | `brazil-runtime-exec <cmd>` |\n| Run cmd in runtime env (Peru) | `brazil-farm -p <pkg> -c <closure>` |\n\n## brazil-context\n\nPrefer `brazil-context` over `findup` and `get-current-platform` — it runs faster by avoiding unnecessary loading of version set files. However, some queries need to load the version set, and that is where `brazil-path` is useful.\n\nNote: `brazil-context package` subcommands must be run from inside a package directory.\n\nSee **[CLI Commands](references/cli-commands.md)** for the full `brazil-context` command listing.\n\n## brazil-path Quick Reference\n\nMUST run from inside a package directory. Syntax: `brazil-path <graph>.<recipe>`\n\nSee **[CLI Commands](references/cli-commands.md)** for common recipes. For anything beyond those, run `brazil-path --help` or `brazil-path -list` from inside a package directory, or see https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/docs/brazil/user-guide/tools-brazil-path/\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Task | Command |\n|------|---------|\n| Create workspace | `brazil workspace create --name NAME --versionSet VS/name` |\n| Use package | `brazil workspace use --package PackageName` |\n| Show workspace | `brazil workspace show` |\n| Build package | `brazil-build release` |\n| Run tests | `brazil-build test` |\n| Clean build | `brazil-build clean` |\n| Fix conflicts | `gk-fix-mv-conflicts` |\n| Sync metadata | `brazil ws sync -md` |\n| Merge dependencies | `brazil workspace merge` |\n| Print version set | `brazil versionset print --versionSet NAME` |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n### Building from Workspace Root\n**Problem:** Running `brazil-build` from workspace root instead of package directory.\n**Fix:** For a single package, always `cd src/PackageName` before building. To build all packages, run `brazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build` from anywhere in the brazil workspace.\n\n### Missing Dependencies After Config Changes\n**Problem:** Added dependencies to Config but build fails with \"not in version set\" errors.\n**Fix:** First try `brazil ws sync -md` to refresh metadata and to sync the metadata to the TIP of the version set. If that doesn't resolve it, run `brazil workspace merge` to submit a merge build. Make it clear to the user that they will need to merge the package into the version set themselves long-term.\n\n### Incorrect or unsure which Major Version to use\n**Problem:** Added dependencies to Config but unsure which major version to use.\n**Fix:** Use ReadInternalWebsite on https://code.amazon.com/packages/PackageName/releases to find available Major Versions\n\n### Ignoring Build Output Volume\n**Problem:** Verbose build output overwhelms context, degrading conversation quality.\n**Fix:** Redirect to log file: `brazil-build release > build.log 2>&1 ; tail -n 50 build.log`\n\n### Resolves Not Working\n**Problem:** `resolves-conflict-dependencies` entries are ignored.\n**Fix:** Use `gk-fix-mv-conflicts --cleanup` to rebuild conflict resolutions, or ensure resolved version exists in dependency graph.\n\n### Wrong Build System Commands\n**Problem:** Using language-specific commands without checking build system.\n**Fix:** Check Config file `build-system` field, then reference appropriate language guide.\n\n### Gordian Knot Not Installed\n**Problem:** `gk-fix-mv-conflicts` command not found.\n**Fix:** Install Gordian Knot via toolbox: `toolbox install gordian-knot`\n\n### Confusing ws merge and vs merge\n**Problem:** Using `brazil vs merge` when intending to update local workspace, or vice versa.\n**Fix:** `brazil ws merge` updates your local workspace's version set (by submitting a merge build). `brazil vs merge` modifies the shared remote version set — use with caution.\n\n## Support Resources\n- **Documentation**: https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/\n- **CTI**: Developer Tools | Brazil\n- **Email**: brazil-users@amazon.com\n- **Package Search**: https://code.amazon.com/packages/search\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /local/home/scheschb/.codex/plugins/cache/aim/AmazonBuilderCoreAIAgents-core/1.0.6505391367/skills/brazil/references/language/rust.md; printf '\\\\n-- workspace inventory --\\\\n'; rg --files -g 'Cargo.toml' -g 'Config' -g 'CMakeLists.txt' -g '*.h' -g '*.c' -g '*.rs' | sed -n '1,240p'; printf '\\\\n-- root --\\\\n'; ls -la; printf '\\\\n-- translated crate --\\\\n'; ls -la translated_rust\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /local/home/scheschb/.codex/plugins/cache/aim/AmazonBuilderCoreAIAgents-core/1.0.6505391367/skills/brazil/references/language/rust.md; printf '\\\\n-- workspace inventory --\\\\n'; rg --files -g 'Cargo.toml' -g 'Config' -g 'CMakeLists.txt' -g '*.h' -g '*.c' -g '*.rs' | sed -n '1,240p'; printf '\\\\n-- root --\\\\n'; ls -la; printf '\\\\n-- translated crate --\\\\n'; ls -la translated_rust\"","aggregated_output":"# Rust with CargoBrazil\n\nCargoBrazil integrates Rust with Amazon's Brazil build system, managing toolchains and dependencies while preserving standard Cargo workflows.\n\n## Quick Start\n\n### Create a New Rust Package\n\nUse CreateHub to generate from template:\n```bash\n# Via toolbox\ntoolbox install create-pkg\ncreate-pkg --template DuplicatorRustPackage --name MyPackage\n\n# Or via CreateHub web UI\n# https://createhub.corp.amazon.com/templates/DuplicatorRustPackage\n```\n\nThis creates a properly configured package with Config, Cargo.toml, and .gitignore already set up.\n\n### Essential .gitignore\n\n```gitignore\n# Generated by CargoBrazil - never commit\nCargo.lock\n/build\ntarget/\n/rust-toolchain.toml\n\n# IDE artifacts\n.idea/\n*.iml\n```\n\n### First Build\n\n```bash\nbrazil ws create --root --vs YourVersionSet\ncd src/YourPackage\nbrazil-build sync      # Setup (run after clone or metadata changes)\nbrazil-build release   # Full build: tests + lints + docs\n```\n\nAfter first build, standard `cargo` commands work directly:\n```bash\ncargo build            # Fast iteration\ncargo test             # Run tests\ncargo clippy           # Linting\ncargo fmt              # Format code\n```\n\n## Workspace Structure\n\n### Single Crate Package\n\n```\nMyPackage/\n├── Config             # Brazil configuration\n├── Cargo.toml         # Rust manifest\n├── src/\n│   └── lib.rs         # or main.rs for binary\n└── build-tools/bin/   # Optional: build scripts\n```\n\n### Multi-Crate Workspace\n\n```\nMyPackage/\n├── Config\n├── Cargo.toml         # Workspace manifest\n├── crates/\n│   ├── core/\n│   │   ├── Cargo.toml\n│   │   └── src/\n│   └── cli/\n│       ├── Cargo.toml\n│       └── src/\n└── build-tools/bin/\n```\n\nWorkspace Cargo.toml:\n```toml\n[workspace]\nresolver = \"2\"\nmembers = [\"crates/*\"]\n\n[workspace.package]\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[workspace.dependencies]\n# Shared dependencies with versions\nserde = { version = \"1.0\", features = [\"derive\"] }\ntokio = { version = \"1.0\", features = [\"full\"] }\n```\n\n## Dependencies\n\n### Third-Party (crates.io)\n\nAdd directly to `Cargo.toml` - no Config or BTPT needed:\n```toml\n[dependencies]\nserde = { version = \"1.0\", features = [\"derive\"] }\ntokio = { version = \"1.0\", features = [\"full\"] }\nanyhow = \"1.0\"\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nmockall = \"0.11\"\nproptest = \"1.0\"\n```\n\nDependencies come from internal mirror (ThirdPartyCratesIoIndex), which typically lags 1-3 days behind crates.io.\n\n### First-Party (Amazon packages)\n\n1. Add to Config:\n```\ndependencies = {\n    1.0 = {\n        SomeRustPackage = 1.x;\n    };\n};\n```\n\n2. Reference in Cargo.toml with `amzn-` prefix:\n```toml\n[dependencies]\namzn-some-crate = \"*\"  # Version from Config/version set\n```\n\n**Important:** The `amzn-` prefix is mandatory. CargoBrazil maps this to the actual crate name from the version set.\n\n### Native Libraries\n\nFor C/C++ dependencies (OpenSSL, zlib, etc.):\n\n```\ndependencies = {\n    1.0 = {\n        Openssl = default;\n        Zlib = 1.x;\n    };\n};\n```\n\nCargoBrazil automatically:\n- Sets `PKG_CONFIG_PATH` for discovery\n- Configures linker search paths\n- Sets library-specific env vars (`OPENSSL_LIB_DIR`, `OPENSSL_INCLUDE_DIR`)\n\n**Twisted builds error:**\n```\nDenying call to cc due to possible silent vendoring in crate '<crate>'\n```\n\n**Fix:** The crate is compiling C code instead of using Brazil's library. Add the corresponding native library to your Config dependencies:\n- `openssl-sys` → add `Openssl = default`\n- `curl-sys` → add `Curl = 7.x`\n- `bzip2-sys` → add `Bzip2 = 1.0`\n\n**Not your direct dependency?** Try `brazil ws sync --md` - an upstream library may have fixed it.\n\nFull guide: https://w.amazon.com/bin/view/Rust/Campaigns/TwistedBuilds/\n\n## Publishing a Library\n\nTo make your crate consumable by other packages:\n\n### Step 1: Add Target\n\n```\ntargets = {\n    MyPackage-1.0 = { type = rust; };\n};\n```\n\n### Step 2: Configure Cargo.toml\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"amzn-mypackage\"  # Must use amzn- prefix\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\npublish = [\"brazil\"]\nexclude = ['/build', '/Config', '/build-tools/']\n\n[lib]\nname = \"amzn_mypackage\"  # Underscores for Rust identifier\n```\n\n### Step 3: Verify\n\nAfter building, consumers can add to their Cargo.toml:\n```toml\n[dependencies]\namzn-mypackage = \"*\"\n```\n\n## Common Tasks\n\n### Adding Build Tools\n\nCommon tools to add to your Config `build-tools` section:\n\n**For faster builds:**\n- `Mold = 2.x` - Faster linking (Linux, ~2-5x speedup)\n- `Cargo-nextest = 0.x` - Faster parallel test execution\n\n**For code quality:**\n- `Grcov = 0.8.x` - Test coverage reporting\n\n**Example config:**\n```\nbuild-tools = {\n    0.1 = {\n        CargoBrazil = 1.0;\n        RustLang1x = 1.x;\n        Grcov = 0.8.x;\n        Cargo-nextest = 0.x;\n        Mold = 2.x;\n    };\n};\n```\n\n**Security scanning and unused dependency detection** require additional setup beyond build-tools. See https://w.amazon.com/bin/view/Rust/AuditingDependencies for CargoEevee and cargo-machete configuration.\n\n### Test Coverage\n\n**Setup:**\n1. Add `Grcov = 0.8.x` to build-tools\n2. Run coverage:\n\n```bash\ncargo brazil with-coverage test --workspace  # Run instrumented tests\ncargo brazil with-coverage report            # Generate HTML report\n# Report at: build/private/cargo-target/coverage/\n```\n\n**Require minimum coverage:**\n```toml\n# Cargo.toml\n[package.metadata.cargobrazil]\nmin-line-coverage = 80\n```\n\nBuild fails if coverage drops below threshold.\n\n### Compiler Flags\n\nUse `.cargo/config.toml` (NOT the RUSTFLAGS env var, which CargoBrazil manages):\n\n```toml\n[build]\nrustflags = [\"-C\", \"target-feature=+crt-static\"]\n\n# For specific targets\n[target.x86_64-unknown-linux-musl]\nrustflags = [\"-C\", \"target-feature=+crt-static\"]\n```\n\n**For environment variables in build scripts:** See https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-extend/#setting-environment-variables for injecting custom env vars via configure override.\n\n### Using Build Scripts\n\nBuild scripts (`build.rs`) work normally. For scripts needing Brazil context:\n\n```rust\n// build.rs\nfn main() {\n    // These are set by CargoBrazil during configure\n    println!(\"cargo:rerun-if-env-changed=BRAZIL_BUILD_HOME\");\n    \n    // Access Brazil dependencies via standard methods\n    let openssl_dir = std::env::var(\"OPENSSL_LIB_DIR\")\n        .expect(\"OPENSSL_LIB_DIR not set\");\n}\n```\n\n**Warning:** Do not call `brazil-path` from build scripts. Use environment variables instead.\n\n## Testing\n\n### Faster Tests with Nextest\n\nAfter adding `Cargo-nextest = 0.x` to build-tools:\n\n```bash\ncargo nextest run          # Parallel test execution\ncargo nextest run -j 8     # Control parallelism\n```\n\n### Doc Tests\n\n```bash\ncargo test --doc           # Run doc examples\nbrazil-build build         # Generates docs during build phase\n```\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### Crate/Version Not Found\n\n```bash\ncargo brazil versions-for-crate --package <crate-name>\n```\n\n- **Version not listed** - Mirror lags 1-3 days. Use available version or wait.\n- **Crate not found** - Not synced. Run `brazil ws sync --md && cargo brazil fetch --target all`\n- **1P crate not found** - Missing Config dependency. Add to Config dependencies.\n- **`amzn-*` not resolved** - Missing prefix. Ensure Cargo.toml uses `amzn-` prefix.\n\n### Runtime Errors\n\n**\"envroot\" or \"interpreter\" errors:**\n\nCargoBrazil wraps binaries for Apollo deployment. For other environments:\n\n```bash\n# Local execution: use unwrapped binary\n./build/bin/<binary>.orig\n\n# Or use Brazil runtime wrapper\nbrazil-runtime-exec <binary>\n```\n\n**For Docker/ECS/Lambda:** Two options:\n\n1. **Use `--no-ld-wrapper`** (simplest, but breaks dynamic linking):\n   Create `build-tools/bin/cargo-brazil-on-install`:\n   ```bash\n   #!/usr/bin/env bash\n   set -euo pipefail\n   cargo brazil install --bins --locked --no-ld-wrapper --path .\n   ```\n\n2. **Use ApolloShimSetup** (preserves dynamic linking):\n   Add runtime dependency and configure shim. See https://w.amazon.com/bin/view/ContainerBuild/ApolloShimSetup\n\n### Build Fleet Failures\n\n**\"Works locally, fails on build fleet\":**\n\n- **Rust version mismatch** - Use Cloud Desktop or match RustLang1x version\n- **Missing build-tools** - Add locally-installed tools to Config\n- **Flaky tests** - Use nextest with retries: `--retries 2`\n\n**Full clean and rebuild (wash cycle):**\n```bash\nbrazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages --reverse brazil-build clean\nbrazil ws clean\nbrazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build release\n```\n\n### Dependency Conflicts\n\n**Version mismatch between workspace crates:**\n```bash\ncargo tree -d  # Show duplicates\ncargo tree -i <crate>  # Show inverse dependencies\n```\n\n**Lock file issues:**\n```bash\nrm Cargo.lock\nbrazil-build sync\n```\n\n### Debug Logging\n\n**CargoBrazil build diagnostics:**\n```bash\nbrazil-build -v release    # Debug level\nbrazil-build -vv release   # Trace level\n\n# Or via environment variable\nCARGO_BRAZIL_LOG=amzn_cargo_brazil=debug,cargo_brazil=debug brazil-build\n```\n\n**Your Rust application logs:**\nMost Rust crates use `tracing` or `log` with `RUST_LOG` env var:\n```bash\nRUST_LOG=debug ./build/bin/my-binary\nRUST_LOG=my_crate=debug,other_crate=warn ./build/bin/my-binary\n```\n\n## IDE Setup\n\n### VS Code\n\nInstall extensions:\n- rust-analyzer (required)\n- CodeLLDB (debugging)\n- Even Better TOML (syntax)\n\nAfter `brazil-build sync`, rust-analyzer works automatically.\n\n**Cloud Desktop (CDD/CDM):** rust-analyzer requires additional setup:\n```bash\naxe init builder-tools\n```\nOr configure rust-analyzer server path manually. See https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/ides-vscode/#remote-ssh-on-a-clouddesk-cdm\n\n### RustRover / IntelliJ\n\n1. Open package directory as project\n2. Run `brazil-build sync` first\n3. Trust the project when prompted\n\n**Common issue:** If rust-analyzer can't find deps, run `brazil-build sync`.\n\n## Platform Notes\n\n- **Linux (AL2/AL2023)** - Brazil-managed (RustLang1x). Matches build fleet, recommended.\n- **macOS (Intel)** - Local rustup. Install matching version manually.\n- **macOS (ARM)** - Local rustup. Some deps may need Rosetta.\n- **Cloud Desktop** - Brazil-managed. Best for consistent results.\n\n**macOS setup:**\n```bash\nrustup default stable\nrustup component add clippy rustfmt\n```\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```bash\n# Build phases\nbrazil-build sync      # Setup/refresh environment\nbrazil-build build     # Docs + artifacts only (fast)\nbrazil-build release   # Full: tests + lints + docs + artifacts\nbrazil-build test      # Tests only\nbrazil-build lint      # Clippy + fmt check\n\n# Cargo Brazil utilities\ncargo brazil fetch --target all          # Fetch deps for all platforms\ncargo brazil versions-for-crate -p NAME  # Check crate availability\ncargo brazil print target-dir            # Get cargo target directory\ncargo brazil install --bins              # Install binaries to build/bin/\n\n# Coverage (requires Grcov)\ncargo brazil with-coverage test --workspace\ncargo brazil with-coverage report\n```\n\n---\n\n## Advanced Topics\n\n### CargoBrazil.toml Configuration\n\nHelps tuning CargoBrazil behavior: parallelism, coverage scope, lint targets. Place this file in package root.\n\n```toml\n[build]\n# Parallel compiler frontend (experimental, can cause issues)\nparallel-rustc = false\n\n[build.brazil-fleet]\n# Disable incremental compilation on fleet (recommended - saves memory)\ndisable-incremental = true\n\n[test]\n# Coverage instrumentation scope\n# \"workspace\" (recommended): Faster, only instruments your crates\n# \"all\": Complete coverage including dependencies\ninstrumentation-scope = \"workspace\"\n\n[lint]\n# What to lint: \"default\" (libs/bins) or \"all\" (includes tests/benches)\ncargo-targets = \"default\"\n\n# Features to enable during linting\nfeatures = [\"feature1\", \"feature2\"]\n```\n\n### Cross-Compilation\n\nFor building against different architectures (musl, Android, iOS):\n\n**Setup:**\n1. Add stdlib: `RustStdMusls = 1.0` (for musl targets)\n2. Add cross-linker: `CrossGCC = gunpowder`\n3. Configure `.cargo/config.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[target.x86_64-unknown-linux-musl]\nlinker = \"x86_64-unknown-linux-musl-gcc\"\n```\n\n4. Fetch and build:\n```bash\ncargo brazil fetch --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\ncargo build --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\n```\n\n**Full guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-cross-compile/\n\n### Parallel Build Fleet Builds (Flexible Build)\n\nSpeed up pipelines by running test/lint phases in parallel with build:\n\n```bash\n# Automated setup\ntoolbox registry add 's3://buildertoolbox-registry-bt-rust-registry-us-west-2/tools.json'\ntoolbox install cargo-brazil-migration-tool\ncargo-brazil-migration-tool flexible-build \\\n    --src-pkg MyPkg \\\n    --vs MyVersionSet \\\n    --delegate test \\\n    --delegate lint\n```\n\nThis creates separate packages for test/lint that run in parallel.\n\n**Full guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-build-speedup/\n\n### Environment Variables\n\n- `CARGO_BRAZIL_PREFER_LOCAL=1` - Prefer local over Brazil artifacts (useful for debugging)\n- See [Debug Logging](#debug-logging) for `CARGO_BRAZIL_LOG` and `RUST_LOG` usage\n\n### Error Message Reference\n\n**\"ThirdPartyCratesIoIndex is N days old\"** - Your version set's crates mirror is stale. Merge from live or wait for sync.\n\n**\"amzn-* not found\"** - Missing `amzn-` prefix in Cargo.toml or missing Config dependency.\n\n**\"Could not find runtime environment\"** - Binary using ld-wrapper but no Apollo envroot. Use `.orig` binary or `brazil-runtime-exec`.\n\n**\"GLIBC_2.xx not found\"** - rust-analyzer binary too new for OS. Install via `axe init builder-tools` on AL2/CDD.\n\n**\"lock file interference\"** - Cargo.lock committed to git. Add to .gitignore and delete.\n\n## Resources\n\n- **CargoBrazil Docs:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil/\n- **Dependency Guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-understanding-cargobrazil-dependencies/\n- **Cross-Compile Guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-cross-compile/\n- **Build Speedup Guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-build-speedup/\n- **Extending CargoBrazil:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-extend/\n- **Rust Book:** https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/\n- **Cargo Book:** https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/\n\n-- workspace inventory --\ntranslated_rust/Cargo.toml\ntranslated_rust/src/jserror.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/lib.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/utf.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/regexp.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsdtoa.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsvalue.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsstring.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsdate.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsstate.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jscompile.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsbuiltin.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsrun.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsboolean.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsrepr.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsarray.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsregexp.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsproperty.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsparse.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/json.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsobject.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsnumber.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsmath.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jslex.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsintern.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsgc.rs\ntranslated_rust/src/jsfunction.rs\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/utfdata.h\ntranslated_rust/generated/lib.rs\ntranslated_rust/generated/build.rs\ntranslated_rust/generated/Cargo.toml\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/utf.h\ntranslated_rust/c_src/CMakeLists.txt\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/utf.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/regexp.h\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/regexp.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/opnames.h\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsvalue.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsstring.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsstate.c\ntranslated_rust/c_src/src/jscompile.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsrun.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsrepr.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsregexp.c\ntranslated_rust/c_src/src/jsbuiltin.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsproperty.c\ntranslated_rust/c_src/src/jsboolean.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsparse.c\ntranslated_rust/c_src/src/jsarray.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/json.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsobject.c\ntranslated_rust/c_src/src/astnames.h\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsnumber.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsmath.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jslex.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsintern.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsi.h\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsgc.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsfunction.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jserror.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsdtoa.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsdate.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jscompile.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsbuiltin.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsboolean.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/jsarray.c\ntranslated_rust/transpile-input/src/astnames.h\ntranslated_rust/c_src/src/utfdata.h\ntranslated_rust/c_src/src/utf.h\ntranslated_rust/c_